<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingston, Ontario</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kingston is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ontario">Ontario</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, at the eastern end of Lake Ontario where it flows into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a>. It grew from the French post of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-frontenac">Fort Frontenac</a>, founded in 1673, and was resettled by Loyalists after 1783 as a naval base. From 1841 to 1844 it was the first capital of the Province of Canada, and it lies at the southern end of the Rideau Canal.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingston-ontario.jpg" alt="Kingston, Ontario" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Quintin Soloviev · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingston,_Ontario.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Kingston stands at the north-eastern end of Lake Ontario, at the beginning of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-lawrence-river">St. Lawrence River</a> and the mouth of the Cataraqui River. It lies between the Prince Edward County and Thousand Islands regions and is nicknamed the Limestone City for its many heritage buildings of local limestone. The region is underlain mostly by Ordovician limestone. Its original name, Cataraqui, derives from an Indigenous name for the area, with many suggested meanings, among them the place where the rivers and lake meet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Archaeological evidence suggests people lived in the region as early as the Archaic period, and more permanent encampments began about 900 CE. The group that first occupied the area before the French was probably the Wendat, later displaced by the Haudenosaunee. Samuel de Champlain visited in 1615, and in 1673 the French founded a trading post and military fort at Cataraqui, later called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-frontenac">Fort Frontenac</a>. After the Conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> the site passed to the British, Cataraqui was renamed Kingston, and Loyalists began settling in the 1780s.</p><p>In 1783 Governor Frederick Haldimand directed Major Samuel Holland to lay out a settlement for Loyalists fleeing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a> and to judge whether the site would serve as a naval base, since nearby Carleton Island had been ceded to the Americans. Holland reported that the harbour was good and conveniently placed to command Lake Ontario. During the War of 1812 Kingston, with 2,250 people, was the base of the British fleet on Lake Ontario, which raced the American fleet at Sackets Harbor. The present Fort Henry was built between 1832 and 1836 to defend the Rideau Canal, opened in 1832.</p><p>Lord Sydenham chose Kingston as the first capital of the united <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canadas</a>, and the Parliament of the Province of Canada first met there on 13 June 1841. The city was judged too small and too near the border, and the capital moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in 1844. Kingston's growth then slowed and its national importance declined, though in the early 20th century it housed the Canadian Locomotive Company, the largest locomotive works in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Kingston had 132,485 people at the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. It is home to Queen's University, founded in 1841 under a royal charter, the Royal Military College of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, established in 1876, and St. Lawrence College. It has the largest concentration of federal correctional facilities in Canada, and its heritage register lists 1,211 properties. Fort Henry, garrisoned by the British until 1871, is part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>. John A. Macdonald, Canada's first prime minister, lived in the city.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingston, Ontario” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The capital moved between <b>Kingston</b>, Montreal, Toronto and Quebec City until Queen Victoria chose Ottawa in 1857.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-canada">Province of Canada</a></li></ul></section>
